**1. The organisation of the United Nations (5 marks).** The United Nations Organisation was founded on **24 October 1945**, after the Second World War, to maintain international peace and security and to promote co-operation. Its Charter was signed at San Francisco in June 1945; it had 51 original members and its headquarters is in **New York**. It has six principal organs:
- the **General Assembly**: all member states, each with one vote; it discusses world problems, makes recommendations, votes the budget and admits new members;
- the **Security Council**: 15 members, five of them permanent with the right of veto (the USA, the USSR/Russia, Britain, France and China) and ten elected for two years; it has the main responsibility for peace and security and can take binding decisions, impose sanctions and send peacekeeping forces;
- the **Secretariat**, headed by the **Secretary-General**, which runs the daily work of the organisation;
- the **International Court of Justice** at The Hague, which settles legal disputes between states;
- the **Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC)**, which co-ordinates the economic and social work and the specialised agencies;
- the **Trusteeship Council**, which supervised the trust territories (it suspended its work in 1994).
The UN also has a family of specialised agencies and programmes (FAO, UNESCO, WHO, ILO, UNICEF and others).

**2. The part played by the agencies in African development since 1970 (three agencies, 5 marks each).** (A candidate chooses three; all four are given.)

**(a) FAO (Food and Agriculture Organisation, founded 1945, Rome).** FAO has worked to increase food production and fight hunger: it has supplied improved seeds, fertilisers and tools, trained farmers and extension workers, supported irrigation, livestock and fisheries development, soil conservation and forestry, helped to fight locusts and livestock diseases, and organised food aid and early warning of famine, for example during the Sahel droughts of the 1970s and 1980s. In countries such as Cameroon it has assisted programmes for agriculture and rural development.

**(b) UNICEF (United Nations Children's Fund, 1946, New York).** UNICEF has worked for the survival and welfare of children and mothers: **immunisation** campaigns against measles, polio, tetanus and other diseases, **oral rehydration** against diarrhoea, nutrition programmes, clean water and sanitation, and support for **primary education**, especially of girls. It also helps children in wars, famine and refugee camps.

**(c) UNESCO (United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organisation, 1945, Paris).** UNESCO has supported **education**: literacy campaigns, teacher training, the building of schools and the **Education for All** movement (from 1990); it promotes science and technology; and it protects culture and heritage by listing **World Heritage Sites** in Africa (such as Great Zimbabwe, and the Dja Faunal Reserve in Cameroon) and supporting the preservation of African languages, archives and history, including the *General History of Africa*.

**(d) WHO (World Health Organisation, 1948, Geneva).** WHO has led the fight against disease: the global campaign that led to the **eradication of smallpox** (declared in 1980), vaccination programmes, control of malaria, river blindness (the Onchocerciasis Control Programme in West Africa from 1974), tuberculosis and **HIV/AIDS**, the promotion of **primary health care**, the training of health workers, and the response to epidemics such as **Ebola** in West Africa (2014-2016). The WHO African region was certified free of wild polio in 2020.

**Conclusion.** Through these agencies the UN has helped African states to improve food supply, child health, education and health care, although progress is uneven and depends on the funds, the stability and the policy of each country.
